Steps to execute 

Step-by-Step Setup in a New GCP VM
Step 1 — Create GCP VM
gcloud compute instances create aiops-vm \
  --zone=us-central1-a \
  --machine-type=e2-standard-2 \
  --image-family=debian-12 \
  --image-project=debian-cloud \
  --tags=http-server \
  --boot-disk-size=20GB

Step 2 — SSH into VM
gcloud compute ssh aiops-vm --zone=us-central1-a

Step 3 — Install Required Packages
sudo apt update -y
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip

Step 4 — Setup Project Directory
mkdir ~/aiops_incident_demo && cd ~/aiops_incident_demo
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install flask flask_sqlalchemy scikit-learn pandas requests

Step 5 — Create the Script

Create file:

nano aiops_incident_dashboard.py


Step 6 — Run the App
source venv/bin/activate
python3 aiops_incident_dashboard.py


You’ll see:

 New AIOps Incident Detected!
 * Running on http://0.0.0.0:8080

Step 7 — Open in Browser

Go to: http://<VM-External-IP>:8080

Slack enablement :

Create a Slack App with Incoming Webhook

Go to  https://api.slack.com/apps

Click “Create New App” → From Scratch

App Name: aiops-alerts

Workspace: Select your workspace (e.g., Intern)

Once the app is created:

From the left menu → click “Incoming Webhooks”

Toggle “Activate Incoming Webhooks” → ON

Click “Add New Webhook to Workspace”

Choose your channel (e.g., #all-intern) → Allow

 Slack will now generate a Webhook URL like:

https://hooks.slack.com/services/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


Keep this safe — this URL is what your Flask app uses.

Verify the Webhook URL

From your GCP VM or Cloud Shell:

curl -X POST -H 'Content-type: application/json' \
--data '{"text":"🚨 Test AIOps Alert: Slack integration working successfully!"}' \
https://hooks.slack.com/services/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


 Go to your Slack channel → you should see a test alert message appear.

If not:

Check that the webhook app is installed for that channel.

Ensure your Slack workspace isn’t restricted for app installs (common in company setups).

 Integrate the Webhook into Python Script

In your aiops_incident_dashboard.py, locate this line:

SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL = "https://hooks.slack.com/services/XXXXXXXXX/YYYYYYYYY/ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ"


Replace it with your real Slack webhook URL (from Step 1).

 Run the Application

From your GCP VM:

cd ~/aiops_incident_demo
source venv/bin/activate
python3 aiops_incident_dashboard.py


You’ll see:

🚨 Ticket created: High latency spike detected...
 * Running on http://0.0.0.0:8080

